Recent advancements in deep learning have enabled accurate brain tissue segmentation from MRI scans, yet the resulting probability maps often exhibit site-specific generalization issues, posing challenges for large-scale multi-site studies. This paper introduces a novel pipeline designed to assess the generalization properties of deep learning-based brain tissue segmentators on multi-site MRI datasets. The proposed method employs a multi-class classification approach, treating each voxel in 3D volume data as an instance. It utilizes the resulting site-wise probability maps for assessment, avoiding the need to re-train or test models on new datasets. The pipeline was developed, validated, and tested using data from the Human Connectome Project (HCP) and the Coordinating Center for the Classification of Treatment-Resistant Epilepsy (C5). Results show that the pipeline effectively identifies generalization problems in pre-trained segmentators on large-scale, multi-site datasets through model performance and probability distribution assessments. Furthermore, the methods prove to be a rapid and potent tool for assessing the generalization properties of brain tissue segmentators on multi-site MRI data.

