Conformance checking compares the behavior of defined process models against behavior recorded in event logs. While alignments are a modern technique for analyzing conformance, they traditionally demand complete process executions (traces) or at least prefixes. We propose infix and postfix alignments as an enhancement to existing final infix and postfix alignments. This study examines various performance metrics of these alignments on extensive process executions featuring diverse, realistic process models. The results demonstrate that infix and postfix alignments outperform their final infix and postfix counterparts in both length and semantic detail, while maintaining comparable performance in calculation efficiency. Consequently, these infix and postfix alignments are the optimal choice for practical conformance checking when utilizing complete traces, and they are the only viable option for analyzing incomplete (prefix) executions.

