The objective of self-supervised learning is to enable machines to extract meaningful patterns and structures from vast amounts of unlabeled data. Although numerous methods have been proposed for this purpose, there remains a lack of comprehensive evaluation frameworks and clear guidelines for selecting appropriate techniques for specific tasks. To address this gap, we constructed a benchmark dataset and assessed various self-supervised learning approaches to determine their efficacy in achieving chest X-ray pretraining. Our findings indicate that contrastive learning techniques, such as SimCLR and DINO, significantly outperform other methods like the Cycle Consistent Adversarial Network (CCGAN) and the Inverse Cycle Consistent Generator (ICG). These models were then fine-tuned on the CheXpert dataset to evaluate their performance across multiple chest X-ray analysis challenges. The findings provide valuable insights for selecting optimal self-supervised models and configurations for both research and practical applications in medical image analysis. Ahmed Sharshar conducted all contributions to this study.

